</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jjzhang@nlpr.ia.ac.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in Reinforcement Post-Training (RPT) have substantially improved the mathematical reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs), but it remains unclear how well such gains transfer across languages. In this work, we study cross-lingual mathematical reasoning transfer in English-centric LLMs under controlled multilingual evaluation. We first evaluate a range of English-centric reasoning models on multilingual mathematical reasoning benchmarks and find that transfer is highly variable across initial model choice, target language, and training paradigm. Through controlled comparative experiments, we further find that stronger English-centric initialization does not necessarily lead to stronger relative transfer efficiency, even when it yields higher multilingual reasoning accuracy. Finally, we show that the largest improvement comes from moving beyond English-only supervision: adding a single parallel language yields a substantial gain, while further gains from additional languages are smaller but consistent. Overall, our results highlight the need to evaluate multilingual mathematical reasoning directly, using both absolute performance and relative transfer-efficiency metrics.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in Reinforcement Post-Training (RPT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> have substantially improved the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs), especially on mathematical benchmarks. In particular, Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> has led to strong gains on datasets such as MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and AIME. As these models continue to improve on English reasoning benchmarks, an important question is whether such gains transfer beyond English. Prior work has mainly studied transfer across tasks or modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, while cross-lingual transfer remains less understood.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This question matters for both multilingual use and model analysis. Strong English reasoning performance does not guarantee equally strong performance for multilingual users, especially when users require both correct answers and interpretable reasoning traces in their own language. More broadly, cross-lingual transfer offers a way to probe what English-centric post-training learns: transferable reasoning behavior, language-specific patterns, or some combination of both. This motivates the central question of this paper:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="58.09" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 58.09" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,58.09) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 52.18 C 0 55.44 2.64 58.09 5.91 58.09 L 647.24 58.09 C 650.51 58.09 653.15 55.44 653.15 52.18 L 653.15 5.91 C 653.15 2.64 650.51 0 647.24 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 52.18 C 1.97 54.36 3.73 56.12 5.91 56.12 L 647.24 56.12 C 649.42 56.12 651.18 54.36 651.18 52.18 L 651.18 5.91 C 651.18 3.73 649.42 1.97 647.24 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 17.72 25.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.64em;--ltx-fo-height:1.95em;--ltx-fo-depth:1.39em;font-size:10pt;" height="46.28" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.98)" width="617.69"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.64em;">
<span id="S1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Q)</span><span id="S1.p3.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> <span id="S1.p3.pic1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To what extent does mathematical reasoning acquired through English-centric post-training transfer across languages under controlled multilingual evaluation?</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We answer this question in three stages. We first conduct an observational study over open-source English-centric reasoning models and find that cross-lingual transfer is highly variable across initial model choice, target language, and training paradigm. We then run controlled comparative studies on initialization type, model family, and model size. Across these settings, stronger English-centric initialization does not necessarily correspond to larger relative cross-lingual transfer gains. Finally, we study parallel multilingual supervision and find that it substantially improves transfer, with the largest gain appearing when moving from monolingual to bilingual training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold. First, we provide an exploratory empirical study of cross-lingual mathematical reasoning transfer in English-centric LLMs, using both absolute performance and relative transfer-efficiency metrics. Second, under controlled GRPO-based post-training, we show that transfer varies across initialization type, model family, and model size. Third, we find that parallel multilingual supervision improves transfer in our setting, with the largest observed single-step gain from English-only to bilingual training and smaller but consistently positive gains from additional languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We organize the paper around the following research questions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ1:</span> To what extent do English-centric LLMs transfer mathematical reasoning across languages? (Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Observational Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ2:</span> What factors are associated with stronger or weaker cross-lingual transfer under controlled post-training settings? (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ3:</span> How can cross-lingual transfer be improved effectively? (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Parallel Training Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Observational Study</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ1</span>—<em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">To what extent do English-centric LLMs transfer mathematical reasoning across languages?</em>—we begin with an observational study of open-source reasoning models. This analysis establishes whether cross-lingual transfer is present and examines how it varies with model initialization, target language, and training paradigm.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate open-source reasoning models tuned with either SFT or RLVR, including the Simple-Zoo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, s1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, OpenThinker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guha et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, Open-Reasoner-Zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and DeepSeek-R1-Distill <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> series.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on multilingual mathematical reasoning benchmarks, including the multilingual versions of MATH500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, AIME2024, and AIME2025 from XReasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We also include multilingual GPQA-Diamond <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al., 2023</a>)</cite> from BenchMAX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as an out-of-domain reasoning benchmark. The evaluation includes <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">English(En), Spanish (es), Russian (ru), German (de), French (fr), Bengali (bn), Swahili (sw), Thai (th), Japanese (ja), Chinese (zh), and Telugu (te)</em>—yielding eleven evaluation languages in total.
More details are given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Multilingual Reasoning Benchmarks ‣ Appendix C Evaluation Details and Setup ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02272v2/open_source_model_MTI_and_across_lang_v4.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/148;" width="452" height="148"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-lingual transfer across open-source reasoning models.</span> The <em id="S2.F1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em> panel reports average MTI across four benchmarks and eleven languages. The <em id="S2.F1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em> panel shows average transfer behavior on multilingual MATH500, comparing SFT-tuned and RL-tuned models.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Language-controlled prompting.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we concatenate the language control instructions after the input prompts to make the model generate responses using the same language as the query.
This setup reflects multilingual application scenarios where both the answer and the reasoning trace should be understandable in the user’s language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. The full prompts are provided in Appendix <a href="#A8.SS2" title="H.2 Multilingual Reasoning Instruction ‣ Appendix H Prompts Template ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.2</span></a> and <a href="#A8.SS3" title="H.3 Prompt hacking to force response language ‣ Appendix H Prompts Template ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report reasoning accuracy (<span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc</span>) as the main task metric, and off-target rate (<span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Off-target</span>) to measure how often a model fails to respond in the requested language, using the LangDetect library. In all evaluation settings, correctness is determined from the final answer only. Intermediate reasoning traces are not scored. Therefore, a model does not receive higher accuracy simply for producing its reasoning trace in the target language.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify relative transfer, we also use the <em id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multilingual Transferability Index</em> (MTI), following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huan et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2026)</a></cite>. MTI is intended to measure how much of the gain obtained in the training language transfers to unseen languages. Since base difficulty varies across languages and benchmarks, absolute accuracy alone is not sufficient for this purpose.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>trained</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the accuracy of a trained model and its corresponding base model on benchmark <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> and language <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the relative gain on language <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R_{b,l}=\frac{Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}{Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>trained</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R_{b,l}=\frac{Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}{Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For a training language set <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the average relative gain is</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R_{b,\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}|}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}\Delta R_{b,l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R_{b,\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}|}\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}\Delta R_{b,l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We define the MTI for an unseen language <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{unseen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>unseen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{unseen}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the average benchmark-level relative transfer ratio:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MTI}_{l_{\text{unseen}}}=\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{b\in B}\frac{\Delta R_{b,l_{\text{unseen}}}}{\Delta R_{b,\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>MTI</mtext><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>unseen</mtext></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>unseen</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MTI}_{l_{\text{unseen}}}=\frac{1}{|B|}\sum_{b\in B}\frac{\Delta R_{b,l_{\text{unseen}}}}{\Delta R_{b,\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An MTI of 1 indicates that the relative gain on an unseen language matches the average gain on the training language(s); values below 1 indicate weaker relative transfer, and values above 1 indicate stronger relative transfer.
MTI measures relative transfer efficiency rather than absolute multilingual reasoning ability: it captures how much of the training-language gain is reflected in unseen languages.
Thus, a high MTI can still correspond to low absolute accuracy, especially from a low base accuracy.
For this reason, we use MTI only as an auxiliary diagnostic and interpret it together with absolute accuracy, non-English average accuracy, low-resource accuracy, and off-target rate throughout the paper, detailed in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Absolute-Transfer Metrics under Parallel Training ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a robustness check in Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Robustness of Transfer Metrics ‣ Appendix F Detailed Results and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>, we also repeated the main analyses with alternative transfer metrics, including normalized gain and raw gain ratio, and examined the sensitivity of MTI to low-base-accuracy cases. The numerical values differ, but the main qualitative conclusions remain unchanged.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this section is that <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-lingual transfer of mathematical reasoning is highly variable</em>. Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Benchmarks. ‣ 2.1 Setup ‣ 2 Observational Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that strong English reasoning does not reliably yield equally strong multilingual performance, and transfer varies across model initialization, training paradigm, and target language.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model choice matters.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transfer varies substantially across models. Even under matched training settings, multilingual performance differs markedly across languages. For example, SimpleRL-Qwen-7B shows slightly higher transferability than SimpleRL-Qwen-Math-7B, despite using the same training data, training paradigm, and hyperparameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">English performance is not enough.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">High English performance and strong multilingual transfer are related, but not identical. Some models that perform well in English do not maintain equally strong multilingual performance, while others with weaker English starting points show larger relative gains after post-training. English evaluation alone is therefore insufficient for characterizing multilingual transfer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training paradigm matters.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the open-source model comparison, RL-tuned models generally show stronger multilingual performance than SFT-tuned models, especially in lower-resource languages such as <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bn</em>, <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sw</em>, and <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">te</em>. In these languages, SFT-tuned models often show weak or negative transfer, whereas RL-tuned models more often yield positive gains. Because the compared models also differ in initialization, training data, and implementation details, we treat this as an observational pattern rather than a controlled comparison of SFT and RL. A matched SFT-vs.-GRPO comparison on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct is provided in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Matched SFT-vs.-GRPO Comparison ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Target language matters.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transfer is not uniform across languages. Higher-resource languages are generally more stable, whereas lower-resource languages show larger relative variation across models and training paradigms. This suggests that cross-lingual transfer depends not only on English-side training, but also on properties of the target language.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Controlled Comparative Study</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The observational study shows that cross-lingual transfer is highly variable, but open-source model comparisons cannot isolate the sources of this variation because models differ in initialization, data, optimization, and model family. To answer <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>, we conduct controlled studies of initialization type, model family, and model size under matched GRPO-based post-training.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training data.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use a compact training set of 1,000 examples sampled from the MATH training split, following prior work on small but carefully designed reasoning supervision sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Muennighoff et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. All controlled studies in this section use the same training set unless otherwise noted. Details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Training Dataset ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Post-training algorithm.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as our RPT algorithm. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mistral-AI et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2026)</a></cite>, the policy is optimized with a composite reward that combines reasoning accuracy, response format, and language consistency:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\lambda_{1}R_{\text{acc}}+\lambda_{2}R_{\text{format}}+\lambda_{3}R_{\text{lang}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>acc</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>lang</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\lambda_{1}R_{\text{acc}}+\lambda_{2}R_{\text{format}}+\lambda_{3}R_{\text{lang}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{acc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>acc</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{acc}}</annotation></semantics></math> rewards correctness, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{format}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{format}}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages well-formed reasoning traces, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{lang}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>lang</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{lang}}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages responses in the target language. Hyperparameter details are given in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Hyperparameters ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a> and <a href="#A6.SS3.SSS1" title="F.3.1 Reward Hyperparameter Sensitivity ‣ F.3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ Appendix F Detailed Results and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because the objective includes formatting and language-consistency terms, gains should not be interpreted as pure reasoning gains alone; we keep the objective fixed across settings for controlled comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Controlled factors.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study three factors: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">initial model type</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model family</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model size</span>. In each case, all training data and optimization settings are fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Initial Model Type</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first study the effect of initial model type using three Qwen2.5-7B variants: a general base model, an instruction-tuned model, and a math-specialized model. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Initial Model Type ‣ 3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows a trade-off between final multilingual performance and relative transfer efficiency. The instruction model achieves the highest multilingual accuracy after English-only post-training and the lowest off-target rate, but the base and math-specialized models obtain higher MTI. This suggests that stronger final multilingual performance and stronger relative transfer efficiency are not always aligned under our setup. Notably, this observation concerns relative transfer efficiency rather than final multilingual performance, which remains highest for the instruction-tuned model under our setup.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acc</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Off-target</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MTI</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">Qwen2.5-7B-Base</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">12.00</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">11.41</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">-</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">     <math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\looparrowright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↬</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\looparrowright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GRPO on En Data</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">22.45</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">3.12</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">1.95</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">22.45</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">1.43</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">-</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">     <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\looparrowright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↬</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\looparrowright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GRPO on En Data</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">23.51</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">0.94</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">1.23</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">
<th id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">Qwen2.5-Math-7B</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">12.25</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">22.59</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">-</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">     <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\looparrowright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↬</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\looparrowright</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S3.T1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GRPO on En Data</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">19.37</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">9.50</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:1pt 6.3pt;">2.12</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Impact of Initial Model Type.</span> Average Accuracy (%), Off-target rate (%), and MTI across eleven languages on four benchmarks.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Model Family</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next compare Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and Llama3.1-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> under the same English-only GRPO setup to test whether the pattern above persists across model families.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02272v2/impact_of_different_initial_model_qwen_vs_llama_on_math500_v2.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/101;" width="476" height="101"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of model family under controlled post-training.</span> Multilingual reasoning performance on MATH500. <em id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Base</em> denotes the initial model, and <em id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">+GRPO</em> the model after English-only GRPO.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 Model Family ‣ 3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that English-only GRPO improves performance in both English and unseen languages for both families. However, the relative gains differ substantially: although Llama3.1-8B-Instruct starts from weaker English performance than Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, it exhibits larger relative gains across languages. This suggests that cross-lingual transfer is not simply determined by English performance alone. At the same time, this comparison does not isolate a pure family effect, since differences in tokenization, pretraining data, and architecture remain entangled. Notably, this comparison concerns relative gains rather than final multilingual accuracy, and should be interpreted accordingly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Model Size</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We finally compare Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct under the same English-only GRPO setup to study how the observed pattern changes with model scale.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02272v2/impact_of_model_size.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/119;" width="476" height="119"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of model size under controlled English-only post-training.</span>
<math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>Performance denotes the average accuracy gain over the initial model, averaged across English and unseen languages.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.4 Model Size ‣ 3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that the effect of model scale is benchmark-dependent. On multilingual MATH500, the 1.5B model gains more than the 7B model, suggesting greater headroom on the in-domain task. On multilingual AIME24/25, however, the 7B model attains stronger final performance on the harder benchmarks despite smaller gains on MATH500. On multilingual GPQA-Diamond, the smaller model again shows larger improvements, while the 7B model improves only marginally and can even degrade in English. One possible explanation is that the small math-only training set over-specializes the 7B model toward mathematical reasoning, which can hurt out-of-domain scientific QA. Overall, the observed transfer pattern depends on the interaction between initial model strength and benchmark difficulty, rather than on model size alone.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02272v2/scaling_laws_clean_v6_2.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/109;" width="476" height="109"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transfer improves with parallel multilingual supervision.</span>
Each panel shows performance as a function of the number of training languages. The red star denotes the English-only baseline (<math id="S3.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1</annotation></semantics></math>), shown for reference but excluded from the fit. Blue points denote multilingual configurations (<math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>). The solid red curve shows a power-law-like descriptive fit over <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, with the shaded region indicating the <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence interval. The fit summarizes the bilingual-to-multilingual trend; Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a> provides the marginal-gain analysis.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Parallel Training Study</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The controlled comparative results suggest that cross-lingual transfer in English-centric post-training is unstable and sensitive to initialization and alignment choices. We therefore ask <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">RQ3</span>—<em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How can cross-lingual transfer be improved effectively?</em> In this section, we study a simple intervention: adding multilingual parallel supervision during post-training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our main finding is that the monolingual-to-bilingual transition yields the largest observed single-step improvement. Additional languages provide smaller but positive gains.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct as the main initial model and post-train it with GRPO on the same 1,000 English MATH examples used in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, together with increasing numbers of parallel languages. The parallel problem sets are drawn from the multilingual MATH dataset described in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Multilingual Reasoning Benchmarks ‣ Appendix C Evaluation Details and Setup ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>, covering seven additional languages: <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">es, ru, de, fr, bn, th, zh</em>. We validate the multilingual data with Qwen3-32B to screen for translation errors, with details and summary statistics reported in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Multilingual Reasoning Benchmarks ‣ Appendix C Evaluation Details and Setup ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>. By varying the number of added languages from one to seven, we obtain a sequence of models that differ only in the amount of multilingual supervision. The full language configurations are given in Table <a href="#A6.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ F.4.2 Parallel Training Language Settings ‣ F.4 Parallel Training Results ‣ Appendix F Detailed Results and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate these models on multilingual MATH500 across eleven languages, using absolute accuracy as the primary metric and MTI as an auxiliary measure of relative transfer efficiency. Unless otherwise stated, this section focuses on multilingual MATH500, which provides the clearest view of the monolingual-to-bilingual transition in our setup.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Power of Bilingual Training</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most direct pattern in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.4 Model Size ‣ 3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is that the largest observed single-step gain occurs when moving from English-only to bilingual parallel training. Using Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct as the initial model, average accuracy increases from 54.24 to 57.87, while MTI increases from 1.16 to 2.50. Importantly, this improvement is not limited to MTI. As shown in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Absolute-Transfer Metrics under Parallel Training ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>, the same transition improves non-English average accuracy from 51.74 to 55.82, reduces the English–non-English gap from 27.46 to 22.58, and improves low-resource average accuracy from 29.53 to 36.27.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We refer to this observed pattern as the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First-Parallel Leap</span>. This term denotes an empirical marginal-gain pattern in our setting: the first added parallel language accounts for the largest single-step improvement, while additional languages continue to provide smaller but positive gains. Thus, moving beyond English-only supervision is especially important, while multilingual benefits do not saturate after one added language.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Beyond Bilingual: Positive but Diminishing Returns</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the bilingual setting, performance continues to improve as more parallel languages are added, but the gains are smaller. Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.4 Model Size ‣ 3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows a smooth sub-linear trend in both MTI and average accuracy. To summarize this pattern, we fit a power-law-like function</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)=\alpha X^{\beta}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)=\alpha X^{\beta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of training languages. We fit only configurations with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> to separate the monolingual and multilingual regimes. The resulting fits are:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textit{Accuracy:}\quad f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Accuracy:</mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textit{Accuracy:}\quad f(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=56.98X^{0.02}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>56.98</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>0.02</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=56.98X^{0.02},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E6Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textit{Transferability:}\quad f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Transferability:</mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textit{Transferability:}\quad f(X)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2.00X^{0.29}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2.00</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>0.29</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2.00X^{0.29}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use this <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Parallel Scaling Trend</span> as a descriptive summary of the bilingual-to-multilingual regime, rather than as a universal scaling law. The sub-linear exponents indicate diminishing returns as more languages are added. To characterize the monolingual-to-bilingual transition, we rely primarily on observed marginal gains: the largest single-step improvement occurs when moving from English-only to bilingual parallel training, while additional languages continue to yield smaller but positive gains. Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a> compares alternative functional forms and reports influence diagnostics, supporting the use of the power-law-like curve as a compact descriptive fit for the multilingual regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Additional Analysis and Discussion</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fixed training budget.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A possible alternative explanation is that parallel training helps simply because it uses more data. To test this, we fix the total number of training examples and vary only the composition. Specifically, we compare scaling English-only data (e.g., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> English examples) with replacing part of it by parallel multilingual data (e.g., <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> English + <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> Russian). Here, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3N</annotation></semantics></math> denote matched budgets of unique training examples rather than repeated epochs over the same <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> examples. Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Fixed training budget. ‣ 4.2 Additional Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4 Parallel Training Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that multilingual mixtures yield stronger cross-lingual transfer than monolingual scaling alone.</p>
</div>
