<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The impact of the selected parallel language.</span> Multilingual reasoning performance across different single-language parallel training settings based on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reasoning Transfer</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent large reasoning models (LRMs) have been driven by reinforcement-learning-based post-training. OpenAI’s O1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> show that RL can substantially improve mathematical reasoning, especially when combined with verifiable or rule-based rewards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As these models improve, an important question is how well the acquired reasoning behavior transfers beyond the training setting. Prior work has mainly studied this problem across tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, showing that RL-induced gains may not transfer uniformly beyond the training setting.
Our work is complementary to this line of research: instead of cross-task or cross-modal transfer, we focus on cross-lingual transfer of mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-Lingual Transfer</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-lingual transfer in English-centric LLMs has been widely studied in multilingual NLP, alignment, and reasoning settings. Prior work shows that English-trained reward models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, preference alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and lightweight multilingual adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chirkova and Nikoulina, 2024</a>)</cite> can often generalize beyond English. In multilingual reasoning, recent studies examine model merging for mathematical capability transfer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bandarkar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, cross-lingual test-time scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, response-language control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, language–reasoning disentanglement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and RL training on non-English data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Parallel multilingual data has also been shown to improve multilingual capability in prompting and pretraining settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qorib et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Our work is complementary: we study cross-lingual transfer in reasoning-oriented post-training, compare same-problem parallel supervision with non-parallel multilingual exposure, and analyze how transfer changes as the number of parallel training languages increases.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study cross-lingual mathematical reasoning transfer in English-centric LLMs under multilingual evaluation. Our results show that transfer exists but is highly variable, and that English reasoning performance alone does not reliably predict multilingual performance. Through controlled GRPO-based experiments, we find that stronger initialization can improve final multilingual accuracy without necessarily improving relative transfer efficiency. We further show that parallel multilingual supervision improves transfer in our setting, with the largest observed single-step gain from English-only to bilingual training. Overall, our findings highlight the need to evaluate multilingual reasoning directly using both absolute performance and relative transfer-efficiency metrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank the anonymous reviewers for their insightful feedback and constructive comments.
This work is supported by Beijing Natural Science Foundation L259016 and the Strategic Priority Research Program of Chinese Academy of Sciences under Grant XDA04080400.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study has several limitations. First, our controlled experiments remain small-scale compared with production-level reasoning post-training, even though we extend the training data from 1K to 3K examples and observe the same qualitative pattern. The results should therefore be interpreted as evidence under our controlled setup rather than as claims about large-scale post-training dynamics. In addition, our controlled experiments are primarily based on GRPO, so they should not be read as a general comparison of all RL-style post-training methods against SFT.
Second, our multilingual evaluation relies on translated benchmarks, which may introduce language-dependent artifacts such as wording shifts, notation mismatch, or uneven difficulty across languages. Evaluation is also conducted under language-controlled prompting, so the reported gains may reflect language compliance and response-format stability in addition to task-solving ability.
Finally, MTI is used only as an auxiliary diagnostic of relative transfer efficiency. It can be unstable in low-accuracy regimes and should not be interpreted as a measure of absolute multilingual reasoning ability. Similarly, the power-law-like trend in the parallel-training study is intended as a descriptive summary of the observed bilingual-to-multilingual regime, rather than as a theoretical scaling law. Overall, our controlled results establish empirical associations rather than a definitive causal mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work studies the cross-lingual transfer of mathematical reasoning in English-centric LLMs. Our contributions are primarily empirical and methodological: we analyze transfer behavior under controlled multilingual evaluation and study training interventions that may improve multilingual reasoning performance. We use publicly available benchmarks that do not contain sensitive personal information to the best of our knowledge. At the same time, multilingual evaluation based on translated benchmarks may overstate or understate performance in some languages if translation artifacts, notation mismatch, or mixed-language mathematical responses are not carefully accounted for. Improvements in response-language control should therefore not be interpreted as complete evidence of robust multilingual reasoning in real-world settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
