<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>GRPO Algorithm</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO is a simplified PPO-based algorithm that significantly reduces training costs by eliminating the need for a value model.
It operates by sampling <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{1},...,o_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{1},...,o_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the current policy for a given input, calculating their cumulative rewards <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\{R_{1},...,R_{G}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\{R_{1},...,R_{G}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then using these rewards to estimate advantages <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> to guide policy updates.
The optimization objective for GRPO is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="A4.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}^{\text{clip}}_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mtext>clip</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}^{\text{clip}}_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min\Big(r_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min\Big(r_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{clip}\big(r_{i,t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\big)\hat{A}_{i,t}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="1.000em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{clip}\big(r_{i,t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\big)\hat{A}_{i,t}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E12Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E12Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E12Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{R_{i}-\operatorname{mean}(R)}{\operatorname{std}(R)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>std</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{R_{i}-\operatorname{mean}(R)}{\operatorname{std}(R)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The clipping term with ratio <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2015</a>)</cite> keeps the new policy close to the old one, improving training stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Training Dataset</h3>

<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Distribution of Parallel Questions</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F8.sf1" title="Figure 8(a) ‣ Figure 8 ‣ The Distribution of non-parallel Questions ‣ D.2 Training Dataset ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a> shows the type and level distributions of the 1,000 English training questions sampled from the MATH dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The type distribution is relatively balanced, and the number of questions increases steadily from Level 1 to Level 5.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Distribution of non-parallel Questions</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, Figure <a href="#A4.F8.sf2" title="Figure 8(b) ‣ Figure 8 ‣ The Distribution of non-parallel Questions ‣ D.2 Training Dataset ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a> presents the type and level distributions of 1,000 Russian questions used for a non-parallel training analysis experiment, which form a separate, non-overlapping set from the 1000 English questions. The distributions of both type and level closely match those of the English training questions. This indicates that, in the analysis comparing parallel and non-parallel training, the performance drop observed in non-parallel training is not due to distributional differences between the non-parallel and English datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02272v2/stat_dataset_type_level_en_1000.svg" id="A4.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/191;" width="476" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.02272v2/stat_dataset_type_level_ru_1000_unparallel.svg" id="A4.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/190;" width="476" height="190"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Distribution of question difficulty. (a) The 1,000 English questions were utilized in the controlled comparative study and for parallel training. (b) The 1,000 Russian questions for non-parallel training, comprising a separate and non-overlapping set from the questions in (a).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Experimental Environments</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training and inference were conducted on Ubuntu 22.04 with 8 <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> NVIDIA A800 GPUs. We use VeRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for RL training, vLLM 0.8.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for inference, and Qwen’s Math evaluation codebase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Hyperparameters</h3>

<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RL Training</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The maximum generation length was set to 4096 tokens, and the maximum prompt length to 1024 tokens, such that their sum matches the model’s maximum context length. The learning rate was fixed at <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Training was performed with a batch size of 128 questions. For each question, <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=16</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts were sampled, using a sampling temperature of 1.0. <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{3}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{3}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inference</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the evaluation setup, we used a temperature of 0.6, a top-<math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> value of 0.95, and a maximum generation length of 8912 tokens for all models in the 1.5B–14B series. For 32B models, we used the same temperature (0.6) and top-<math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> value (0.95), but set the maximum generation length to 32,768 tokens, except for DAPO-Qwen-32B, which followed the official recommended settings: a temperature of 1.0, a top-<math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> value of 0.7, and a maximum generation length of 20,480 tokens. For AIME2024 and AIME2025, we report accuracy by averaging over 16 sampled generations per question, while for MATH500 and GPQA, accuracy is computed using a single sampled generation per question.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Experiments and Analysis</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Matched SFT-vs.-GRPO Comparison</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To complement the observational comparison in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Observational Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we conduct a matched comparison using the same Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct backbone and the same multilingual MATH500 evaluation protocol. We compare the initial model, an SFT model trained on 1K English MATH examples, and a GRPO model trained on the same 1K English MATH examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ E.1 Matched SFT-vs.-GRPO Comparison ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows that GRPO achieves the strongest multilingual performance under this controlled setting. Compared with SFT, GRPO improves average accuracy from 50.80 to 54.24, non-English average accuracy from 48.20 to 51.74, and low-resource average accuracy from 22.13 to 29.53. In contrast, SFT improves English accuracy over the base model but does not consistently improve multilingual performance, and it increases the average off-target rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This comparison supports the observational trend in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Benchmarks. ‣ 2.1 Setup ‣ 2 Observational Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> under a matched backbone and evaluation setting. However, it is limited to one backbone, one training set, and one benchmark, and should therefore not be interpreted as a universal claim that GRPO or RL-style post-training always outperforms SFT.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
