</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span><span id="A5.T7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute-transfer metrics for Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct under parallel training.</span>
All accuracy and gap values are percentages. Non-En denotes average accuracy over non-English languages; Gap denotes the En–Non-En accuracy gap; Low-res. denotes average accuracy over <em id="A5.T7.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bn</em>, <em id="A5.T7.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sw</em>, and <em id="A5.T7.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">te</em>. MTI is reported as an auxiliary relative transfer-efficiency metric.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ E.2 Absolute-Transfer Metrics under Parallel Training ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that the benefit of parallel multilingual supervision is visible not only in MTI, but also in absolute multilingual performance. Moving from English-only training to one added parallel language improves non-English average accuracy from 51.74 to 55.82, reduces the English–non-English gap from 27.46 to 22.58, and improves low-resource average accuracy from 29.53 to 36.27. Adding more parallel languages further improves non-English average accuracy to 57.81 and reduces the English–non-English gap to 18.79 with seven added languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These results support the interpretation that MTI should be read together with absolute performance metrics. A high MTI indicates strong relative transfer efficiency, but it does not by itself imply high absolute multilingual reasoning accuracy. We therefore use MTI as a complementary diagnostic rather than as the sole measure of cross-lingual reasoning transfer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Parallel Training Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we use a power-law-like curve to summarize the diminishing-return trend in the multilingual regime. This curve is intended only as a descriptive fit, not as a theoretical scaling law. To examine whether this descriptive choice is reasonable, we compare four candidate functional forms on the multilingual regime, where the number of training languages is <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=2,\ldots,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=2,\ldots,8</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="A5.E13.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">linear:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(X)=aX+b," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(X)=aX+b,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="A5.E14.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">logarithmic:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(X)=a\log X+b," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(X)=a\log X+b,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="A5.E15.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">power-law:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(X)=aX^{b}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(X)=aX^{b},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="A5.E16.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">saturation:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(X)=c-ae^{-bX}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(X)=c-ae^{-bX}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the fits using <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, leave-one-out cross-validation mean squared error (LOOCV MSE), Akaike information criterion (AIC), and Bayesian information criterion (BIC). LOOCV MSE measures out-of-sample predictive error, while AIC and BIC compare fit quality with penalties for model complexity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.T8.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
