<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span><span id="A5.T8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Curve-fitting comparison for MTI in the multilingual regime.</span>
Fits are evaluated on configurations with <math id="A5.T8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=2,\ldots,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=2,\ldots,8</annotation></semantics></math>. MSE denotes the leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) MSE.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the power-law fit achieves the best LOOCV MSE, AIC, and BIC for both Qwen and Llama MTI, with competitive <math id="A5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We therefore use the power-law-like curve as a compact descriptive summary of the multilingual regime, rather than as evidence for a universal scaling law.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also examine the role of the monolingual point <math id="A5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1</annotation></semantics></math> under an all-point fit. As an influence diagnostic, we fit the power-law curve on all observed points <math id="A5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1,\ldots,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1,\ldots,8</annotation></semantics></math> and compute Cook’s distance for each point.</p>
</div>
