<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span><span id="A5.T9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cook’s distance under all-point power-law fits.</span>
The power-law curve is fit to all observed points, <math id="A5.T9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1,\ldots,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1,\ldots,8</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows that <math id="A5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1</annotation></semantics></math> has the largest Cook’s distance in all four settings. It also exceeds the common <math id="A5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4/n</annotation></semantics></math> rule-of-thumb threshold, which is <math id="A5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math>. We treat this result as an influence diagnostic: it suggests that the monolingual point is influential under an all-point power-law fit, but it is not a formal statistical test of a separate regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we complement the curve-fitting analysis with a direct marginal-gain analysis. Table <a href="#A5.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> reports the gain from each consecutive increase in the number of training languages.</p>
</div>
