<tr id="A5.T10.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T10.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A5.T10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\rightarrow 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\rightarrow 6</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A5.T10.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+0.40</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+0.42</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+2.20</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+0.193</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T10.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T10.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A5.T10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\rightarrow 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\rightarrow 7</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A5.T10.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+0.40</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+0.54</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+1.20</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">+0.059</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T10.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T10.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="A5.T10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\rightarrow 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\rightarrow 8</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A5.T10.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">+0.10</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">+0.09</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">-0.07</td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">+0.097</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span><span id="A5.T10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Marginal-gain decomposition over consecutive training-language counts.</span>
Each row reports the gain from increasing the number of training languages from <math id="A5.T10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.T10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X+1</annotation></semantics></math> in the Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct parallel-training experiments.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows that the <math id="A5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\rightarrow 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\rightarrow 2</annotation></semantics></math> transition yields the largest consecutive gain across average accuracy, non-English average accuracy, low-resource average accuracy, and MTI. Additional languages continue to provide gains on most metrics, but the marginal improvements are smaller.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We further summarize how much of the total <math id="A5.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1\rightarrow 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1\rightarrow 8</annotation></semantics></math> gain is explained by the first added parallel language in Table <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T11" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T11.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.T11.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T11.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="A5.T11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric</span></th>
<th id="A5.T11.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><math id="A5.T11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1\rightarrow 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟐</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1\rightarrow 2}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A5.T11.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><math id="A5.T11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1\rightarrow 8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟖</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1\rightarrow 8}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A5.T11.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="A5.T11.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fraction</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.T11.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T11.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">Avg Acc.</th>
<td id="A5.T11.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+3.70</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+5.30</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">69.8%</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T11.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T11.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">Non-En Acc.</th>
<td id="A5.T11.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+4.08</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+6.07</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">67.2%</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T11.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T11.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">Low-res. Acc.</th>
<td id="A5.T11.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+6.73</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+10.93</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">61.6%</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T11.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T11.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">MTI</th>
<td id="A5.T11.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+1.333</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">+2.468</td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">54.0%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span><span id="A5.T11.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fraction of total gain explained by the first added parallel language.</span>
The table compares the gain from English-only to bilingual training
(<math id="A5.T11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\rightarrow 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\rightarrow 2</annotation></semantics></math>) with the total gain from English-only to eight-language
training (<math id="A5.T11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\rightarrow 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\rightarrow 8</annotation></semantics></math>). Avg Acc. denotes average accuracy over all
languages; Non-En Acc. denotes average accuracy over non-English languages;
Low-res. Acc. denotes average accuracy over <em id="A5.T11.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bn</em>, <em id="A5.T11.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sw</em>, and <em id="A5.T11.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">te</em>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Together, Tables <a href="#A5.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ E.3 Functional-Form Comparison and Marginal-Gain Analysis ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> show that the English-only to bilingual transition is the largest observed consecutive improvement and explains a large fraction of the total improvement from <math id="A5.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=8</annotation></semantics></math>: 69.8% for average accuracy, 67.2% for non-English average accuracy, 61.6% for low-resource average accuracy, and 54.0% for MTI. Additional languages continue to provide smaller but generally positive gains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>Parallel vs. Non-Parallel Multilingual Supervision</h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further examine whether the bilingual gain comes from same-problem cross-lingual alignment rather than multilingual exposure alone, we compare parallel bilingual supervision with non-parallel multilingual supervision. We compute the advantage of parallel training as</p>
<table id="A5.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Advantage}=Acc_{\text{parallel}}-Acc_{\text{non-parallel}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Advantage</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>parallel</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>non-parallel</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Advantage}=Acc_{\text{parallel}}-Acc_{\text{non-parallel}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for each benchmark-language pair.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T12" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T12.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.T12.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="A5.T12.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group</span></th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><math id="A5.T12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="A5.T12.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean Adv.</span></th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;"><span id="A5.T12.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">W/T/L</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.T12.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">Overall</th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">44</th>
<td id="A5.T12.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">+0.995</td>
<td id="A5.T12.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">28 / 8 / 8</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T12.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">Non-English</th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">40</th>
<td id="A5.T12.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">+1.101</td>
<td id="A5.T12.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">28 / 5 / 7</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T12.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">Unseen Non-En</th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">36</th>
<td id="A5.T12.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">+1.213</td>
<td id="A5.T12.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">27 / 5 / 4</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T12.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">Unseen low-res.</th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">12</th>
<td id="A5.T12.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">+1.632</td>
<td id="A5.T12.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">9 / 3 / 0</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T12.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">Added lang. (ru)</th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">4</th>
<td id="A5.T12.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">+0.093</td>
<td id="A5.T12.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">1 / 0 / 3</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T12.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T12.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">English</th>
<th id="A5.T12.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">4</th>
<td id="A5.T12.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">-0.063</td>
<td id="A5.T12.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:8.0pt;padding-right:8.0pt;">0 / 3 / 1</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span><span id="A5.T12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel vs. non-parallel multilingual supervision.</span>
The advantage is computed as
<math id="A5.T12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Acc_{\text{parallel}}-Acc_{\text{non-parallel}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>parallel</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>non-parallel</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Acc_{\text{parallel}}-Acc_{\text{non-parallel}}</annotation></semantics></math>
over benchmark-language pairs. W/T/L denotes wins, ties, and losses.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ E.4 Parallel vs. Non-Parallel Multilingual Supervision ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows that parallel supervision improves accuracy by 0.995 points on average across all benchmark-language pairs and wins on 28 out of 44 pairs. The advantage is larger for unseen non-English languages and unseen low-resource languages, while the added language itself shows only a small mean advantage. This suggests that the gain is not merely localized to the added parallel language, but transfers to other unseen languages as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.5 </span>Additional Analysis of Open-Source Model Transfer</h3>

<div id="A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide additional examples to clarify how Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Benchmarks. ‣ 2.1 Setup ‣ 2 Observational Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> supports the observation that cross-lingual transfer is highly variable across models, training paradigms, and target languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, strong English performance does not necessarily imply strong multilingual performance. For example, on MATH500, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B achieves 86.2% accuracy on English, but only 18.4% on Swahili and 17.6% on Telugu. Similarly, OpenThinker2-7B reaches 86.0% on English, but only 12.6% on Swahili and 8.4% on Telugu.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, transfer can differ substantially even within the same model family. For example, Qwen-2.5-7B-SimpleRL-Zoo and Qwen-2.5-14B-SimpleRL-Zoo are from the same model family, but their MATH500 average accuracies differ substantially, 56.47% versus 63.93%. Their low-resource performance also changes noticeably; for example, Telugu accuracy increases from 25.8% to 41.0%.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Third, Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Benchmarks. ‣ 2.1 Setup ‣ 2 Observational Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> suggests an observational training-paradigm trend: RL-style models have higher average MTI than SFT-style models in our open-source model pool. However, since these models differ in backbone, data, and training recipe, this pattern should be interpreted as observational rather than causal. We therefore use this analysis as motivation for the controlled comparisons in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.6 </span>Language-Level Accuracy and Relative Variance</h3>

<div id="A5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further analyze whether languages with lower average accuracy also exhibit larger relative variation across models. For each benchmark and language, we compute the mean accuracy and coefficient of variation (CV) across open-source models:</p>
<table id="A5.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CV}=\frac{\mathrm{std}}{\mathrm{mean}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CV</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>std</mi><mi>mean</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CV}=\frac{\mathrm{std}}{\mathrm{mean}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We use CV instead of raw standard deviation to account for differences in average accuracy across languages.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T13" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T13.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.T13.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T13.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="A5.T13.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scope</span></th>
<th id="A5.T13.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="A5.T13.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pearson <math id="A5.T13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="A5.T13.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;"><span id="A5.T13.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spearman <math id="A5.T13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.T13.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T13.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">Overall</th>
<td id="A5.T13.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.956</td>
<td id="A5.T13.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.882</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T13.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T13.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">MATH500</th>
<td id="A5.T13.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.971</td>
<td id="A5.T13.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.946</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T13.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T13.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">AIME24</th>
<td id="A5.T13.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.901</td>
<td id="A5.T13.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.946</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T13.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T13.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">AIME25</th>
<td id="A5.T13.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.948</td>
<td id="A5.T13.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.936</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T13.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T13.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">GPQA-Diamond</th>
<td id="A5.T13.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.777</td>
<td id="A5.T13.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:10.0pt;padding-right:10.0pt;">-0.727</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 13: </span><span id="A5.T13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correlation between mean accuracy and relative variance across languages.</span>
For each benchmark and language, mean accuracy and CV are computed across open-source models. Negative correlations indicate that lower-accuracy languages tend to have higher relative variance.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ E.6 Language-Level Accuracy and Relative Variance ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> shows a strong negative correlation between language-level mean accuracy and CV across benchmarks. This indicates that lower-accuracy languages tend to exhibit higher relative variation across models. At the group level, low-resource languages (<em id="A5.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bn</em>, <em id="A5.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sw</em>, and <em id="A5.SS6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">te</em>) also have a higher mean CV than the other languages (0.956 vs. 0.706). These results suggest that the observed target-language variation reflects not only language identity or resource level, but also higher relative instability in low-accuracy regimes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Detailed Results and Analysis</h2>

<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Robustness of Transfer Metrics</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine whether our conclusions depend on the specific choice of MTI, we repeated the main analyses using two alternative transfer metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we consider a normalized-gain variant:</p>
<table id="A6.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta N_{b,l}=\frac{Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}{1-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>trained</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta N_{b,l}=\frac{Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}}{1-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and define the corresponding transfer ratio by replacing <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta N</annotation></semantics></math> in the MTI computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we consider a raw-gain ratio:</p>
<table id="A6.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta G_{b,l}=Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>trained</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta G_{b,l}=Acc^{\text{trained}}_{b,l}-Acc^{\text{base}}_{b,l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and compare the gain on an unseen language with the average gain on the training language(s):</p>
<table id="A6.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RG}_{b,l_{\text{unseen}}}=\frac{\Delta G_{b,l_{\text{unseen}}}}{\Delta G_{b,\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>RG</mtext><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>unseen</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>unseen</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RG}_{b,l_{\text{unseen}}}=\frac{\Delta G_{b,l_{\text{unseen}}}}{\Delta G_{b,\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A6.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ F.1 Robustness of Transfer Metrics ‣ Appendix F Detailed Results and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> summarizes the corresponding results on the main parallel-training comparison. Although the absolute values differ across metrics, the main qualitative pattern remains unchanged: moving from English-only to bilingual training yields the largest gain, and adding another parallel language provides a smaller additional improvement. This supports the main-text conclusion that the First-Parallel Leap is not specific to the original MTI definition.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T14" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A6.T14.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A6.T14.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T14.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;"><span id="A6.T14.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setting</span></th>
<th id="A6.T14.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;"><span id="A6.T14.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MTI</span></th>
<th id="A6.T14.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;"><span id="A6.T14.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NGR</span></th>
<th id="A6.T14.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;"><span id="A6.T14.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RGR</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A6.T14.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T14.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">En-only</th>
<td id="A6.T14.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">1.163</td>
<td id="A6.T14.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">0.396</td>
<td id="A6.T14.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">0.732</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T14.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T14.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">+1 parallel</th>
<td id="A6.T14.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">2.496</td>
<td id="A6.T14.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">0.963</td>
<td id="A6.T14.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">1.480</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T14.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T14.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">+2 parallels</th>
<td id="A6.T14.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">2.650</td>
<td id="A6.T14.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">1.061</td>
<td id="A6.T14.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.75pt 10.0pt;">1.601</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 14: </span><span id="A6.T14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness of transfer metrics on the main parallel-training comparison.</span>
We compare the original MTI with normalized-gain ratio (NGR) and raw-gain
ratio (RGR). All three metrics show a large gain from English-only to
one-parallel training, followed by a smaller additional gain.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sensitivity to base accuracy.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because MTI is based on relative gain, it can become sensitive when base accuracy is low. To assess whether the main result is driven by such cases, we recompute the transfer scores on multilingual MATH500 after excluding unseen-language cases whose base accuracy falls below different thresholds. Table <a href="#A6.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ Sensitivity to base accuracy. ‣ F.1 Robustness of Transfer Metrics ‣ Appendix F Detailed Results and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> shows that the absolute values vary across metrics and filters, especially for the original relative-gain MTI. However, the main qualitative result remains unchanged: the transition from monolingual to bilingual training yields a clear improvement under all transfer metrics and filtering thresholds. This suggests that the bilingual gain is not an artifact of the lowest-base-accuracy cases alone.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T15" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A6.T15.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A6.T15.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T15.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.4pt;padding-right:3.4pt;"><span id="A6.T15.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setting</span></th>
