</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="A6.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Impact of Different Model Families in Controlled Comparative Study.</span> Multilingual reasoning performance across languages, comparing the influence of model family using Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Llama3.1-8B-Instruct as initial models.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A6.SS3.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-lingual Transfer within the Sino-Tibetan Family.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To complement the model family comparison, we also evaluate Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on several Sino-Tibetan languages after English-only GRPO. Table <a href="#A6.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ Cross-lingual Transfer within the Sino-Tibetan Family. ‣ F.3.3 Detailed Results for Model Family ‣ F.3 Controlled Comparative Study ‣ Appendix F Detailed Results and Analysis ‣ How Well Does Mathematical Reasoning Transfer Across Languages in English-Centric LLMs?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> shows that Qwen retains higher absolute accuracy in Chinese, while Llama exhibits larger relative gains on lower-performing languages such as Tibetan and Myanmar. We report this result as an additional case study consistent with the main-text observation that stronger starting performance and larger relative gains do not always coincide.</p>
</div>
